---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Keep this page in step with what the site actually collects. If something
// changes (a new form, analytics, another service), update it here too.
const updated = 'November 4, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How dianemelton.com handles your information: newsletter signups, comments, and the few things stored in your browser."
  minimalNav={true}
>
  <section class="privacy-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p>Last updated {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="privacy">
        <p class="privacy__lead">
          This site belongs to me, Diane Melton (Diane F Melton LLC). I collect as little as I can, I don't
          sell or share your information for advertising, and the site doesn't use advertising or tracking cookies.
          Here is exactly what happens.
        </p>

        <h2>If you sign up for my newsletter</h2>
        <p>I keep:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>your <strong>email address</strong>, so I can send you the newsletter;</li>
          <li>
            <strong>how you found the site</strong>: the website that sent you here (for example Google or
            Facebook, but never what you searched for), any campaign tag on the link you followed, the page you
            first arrived on, and which signup form you used. This helps me understand which places people
            discover the book through.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          My newsletter is sent through <a href="https://www.mailerlite.com/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MailerLite</a>,
          which stores this information on my behalf. Like most email services, MailerLite reports whether a
          newsletter was opened and which links were clicked, so I can see what readers find useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every newsletter has an <strong>unsubscribe</strong> link. Unsubscribing stops all emails straight away.
        </p>

        <h2>If you leave a comment</h2>
        <p>
          I keep the <strong>name</strong> you enter and your <strong>comment</strong>. Comments appear on the site
          only after I approve them, and I receive an email letting me know a new one is waiting. I don't ask for
          your email address to comment.
        </p>
        <p>
          To stop spam, your <strong>IP address</strong> is used to limit how many comments can be posted in a
          short time. It is held for ten minutes and is not saved with your comment.
        </p>

        <h2>What's stored in your browser</h2>
        <p>The site keeps a few small notes in your browser's storage. They stay on your device:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>How you arrived</strong> (the referring site, campaign tag and landing page described above),
            kept for 30 days. It is only sent to me if you sign up for the newsletter.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Whether you've subscribed</strong>, so the signup pop-up doesn't keep asking.</li>
          <li><strong>Whether you closed the pop-up</strong>, until you close your browser tab.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can clear these at any time by clearing your browser's data for this site.</p>

        <h2>Services that help run the site</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Vercel</strong> hosts the site and its images. Like any web host, it keeps short-lived server logs that include IP addresses.</li>
          <li><strong>MailerLite</strong> sends the newsletter and stores subscriber details. Newsletter photos on the blog load from MailerLite's servers.</li>
          <li><strong>Upstash</strong> stores blog posts and comments.</li>
          <li><strong>Resend</strong> sends me the email notifications about new comments.</li>
          <li><strong>Google Fonts</strong> provides the site's typefaces, so your browser fetches them from Google.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each of these services handles data under its own privacy policy. None of them receives your information for advertising.</p>

        <h2>Your choices</h2>
        <p>
          You can ask me what I hold about you, ask me to correct it, or ask me to delete it, including removing
          you from the newsletter and taking down a comment. The easiest way is to reply to any of my newsletter
          emails.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>This site isn't directed at children under 13, and I don't knowingly collect their information.</p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>If what the site collects changes, I'll update this page and the date at the top.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy-hero {
    padding: 8rem 0 4rem;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--color-blush) 0%, var(--color-blush-deep) 100%);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .privacy-hero h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-pink-dark);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .privacy-hero p {
    color: var(--color-text-light);
  }

  .privacy {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    color: var(--color-warm-gray);
  }
  .privacy__lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .privacy h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-pink-dark);
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .privacy p { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .privacy ul { margin: 0 0 1rem 1.25rem; }
  .privacy li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .privacy a { color: var(--color-pink); text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .privacy a:hover { color: var(--color-pink-dark); }
</style>
